cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "RawHebb: a language model that learns without back-propagation"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata from the Zenodo DOI minted for the GitHub release you used."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "X005"
    given-names: "X005-0001-0001"
repository-code: "https://github.com/X005-0001-0001/RawHebb"
url: "https://github.com/X005-0001-0001/RawHebb"
abstract: >-
  RawHebb is a pure forward-dynamics language model: leaky membrane integrators,
  threshold spikes and local synaptic plasticity, trained with no optimiser, no loss
  backward pass, no global gradient, no attention, and no hand-tuned magic constants.
  The local rules are the only things that change: a least-squares/Newton output head,
  a recurrent-state associative memory, exact token-pair and hashed-prefix Hebbian
  count memories, and per-layer readout weights. The repository ships a single-file
  application plus the full research lineage and its diagnostics. On a held-out
  150-dialogue split (full corpus, one epoch). The 0.18 checkpoint reaches CE 3.0543,
  top-1 41.86% and top-5 64.23% on the last-128-token protocol; 0.20.0.0 scores the same
  checkpoint on full training windows (CE 3.3545 / 38.06% / 60.60%, 166 632 positions), so the
  two protocols are not directly comparable. The model beats a
  window-matched bigram baseline (CE 3.809, top-1 31.32%) on all three metrics. Negative results, the target-shift bug fixed in
  0.10.0.0, and the corrected n-gram reference are documented explicitly.
keywords:
  - biologically plausible neural networks
  - local learning rules
  - Hebbian learning
  - no back-propagation
  - associative memory
  - language modelling
license: AGPL-3.0
version: 0.20.1.0
